  "text": "Question three. What is ETL versus ELT, and why did ELT become the default? ETL, extract, transform, load, transforms the data on its own servers before loading it into the warehouse. ELT, extract, load, transform, loads the raw data first, and transforms it inside the warehouse, in sequel, often with dbt. ELT became the default because cloud warehouses have cheap, elastic compute, so transforming in place is fast. And the raw data stays: when the logic changes, you transform again without extracting again. ETL still fits a target with little compute, or one with a strict schema.\n\nQuestion four. What is change data capture, CDC, and when do you use it? The simple alternative is polling: select the rows whose updated at is later than the last run. Since the last run, order two shipped, order four was placed, and order three was deleted. The poll finds two and four. The deleted row isn't there to select, so the delete never reaches the warehouse. CDC reads the database's write-ahead log instead, where every insert, update and delete is already recorded in order, and passes each change downstream in near real time, often through Kafka, a distributed log. Use it for low-latency replication of transactional data without polling the source table, and whenever deletes must arrive.",
